the proper conversion of farmers’ responses into standard units by providing a visual interface with the respondents during the interview. Similar types of visual aid in the form of laminated cards with pictures have also been applied in paper surveys (see, for instance, the 2010-11 round (wave 3) of the Malawi Integrated Household Survey). However, the construction of comprehensive libraries for all possible non-standard units in each specific region of a country is often a missing input to fully effective CAPI design. Even with proper conversion factors in hand, there continue to be challenges in the measurement of crop production quantities and/or values. We have already referenced the difficulties for farmers to recall events over periods of several weeks or months. One major complication is due to the fact that many of the poorest smallholders consume the largest share of their production, which thus never reaches the market. As a result, quantification and valuation of own-production consumed by the household is particularly challenging, in part due to the fact that farm-gate prices are rarely available in these cases. Lacking farm-gate prices, valuation is generally based on either market prices or unit values derived from the survey by computing an average (or median) value over some geographic area of reference. In
